BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:waag-trust-me-im-an-airtist-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261101T195719Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260930
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261001
SUMMARY:Deadline: Trust me\, I'm an AI/rtist: Open Call for Creative Interv
 entions
DESCRIPTION:Waag Futurelab invites artists\, theatre makers\, choreographer
 s\, dancers\, game designers and other creative practitioners to engage di
 rectly with seven experimental generative AI Enablers being co-created wit
 h technologists and dance\, theatre and gaming professionals. The call is 
 an invitation into the development process: selected artists get access to
  the technologies\, datasets\, interfaces and assumptions that shape these
  systems while they are still being built. Rather than testing whether the
  systems work\, artists are asked to interrogate why they work the way the
 y do\, who they are designed for\, what forms of creativity they enable or
  constrain\, and what assumptions about culture\, intelligence\, authorshi
 p\, labour\, bodies\, audiences and society are embedded in them. Waag is 
 looking for individuals and collectives willing to move beyond adoption in
 to investigation\, and welcomes proposals that are speculative\, critical\
 , poetic\, provocative\, humorous\, performative\, constructive or disrupt
 ive\, including work that challenges\, misuses\, reimagines\, hacks\, subv
 erts or plays with the tools\, exposes hidden biases\, questions environme
 ntal costs\, or imagines alternative technological futures. THE SEVEN AI E
 NABLERS\, each requiring an on-site visit to its technology partner: the G
 eneric Procedural Content Generation Enabler (text-to-3D assets and avatar
 s\, CERTH\, Greece)\; the Data-Driven Cultural Content Adaptation Enabler 
 (audience-aware creative content ideation with ranked candidate ideas\, CE
 RTH\, Greece)\; the Cross-Domain Audience Engagement Analytics Enabler (tu
 rning heterogeneous cultural participation data into interpretable engagem
 ent signals\, The Audience Agency\, Warrington\, UK)\; the Dance Style Ana
 lysis and Generation Enabler (dance video into virtual 3D choreography\, d
 issected into body\, effort\, shape and space components\, Centre of Excel
 lence for Research & Innovation\, Cyprus)\; the Immersive Design Integrati
 on Enabler for Dance & Theatre (NLP-driven XR environments with speech-to-
 3D scene editing\, Cyprus)\; the Music-to-Movement Translation Enabler (au
 dio into a time-aligned feature set spanning genre classification\, emotio
 nal valence-arousal analysis\, rhythm extraction and latent-space projecti
 on\, feeding motion generation\, spatial audio and visual choreography\, A
 ristotle University of Thessaloniki\, Greece)\; and the Adaptive Narrative
  Enabler for Games & Theatre (an evolutionary AI trained on creator-writte
 n story elements that generates infinite story variations\, explicitly rej
 ecting LLMs because of their dependence on copyrighted or public-domain co
 rpora that encode 19th and early 20th century sexism\, racism and imperial
 ism\, CAUSA Creation\, Austria). ELIGIBILITY: artists\, designers\, creati
 ve technologists\, theatre makers\, dancers\, choreographers and game desi
 gners\, as individuals or collectives\; applicants must hold a European VA
 T number. An info session with live demonstrations of the AI Enablers runs
  31 July 2026\, 10:00 to 11:30 CEST (meeting ID 995 7016 2341). Applicatio
 ns go through Homerun using the official Application Template\, uploaded a
 s a PDF\; read the Guide for Applicants\, which contains the full Enabler 
 descriptions\, before applying. Questions: zoenie at waag dot org. The AI 
 Enablers and this call are part of the Horizon Europe project HAMLET\, whi
 ch aims to make generative AI accessible to cultural organisations of all 
 sizes. Closes 30 September 2026\, 23:59 CEST. Free to apply.\n\nOrganisati
 on: Waag Futurelab (Horizon Europe project HAMLET)\nLocation: Europe. Enga
 ging with a specific AI Enabler includes an on-site visit to its technolog
 y partner: CERTH (Greece)\, The Audience Agency (Warrington\, UK)\, the Ce
 ntre of Excellence for Research & Innovation (Cyprus)\, Aristotle Universi
 ty of Thessaloniki (Greece)\, or CAUSA Creation (Austria). Final presentat
 ions September 2027.\nAward: EUR 20\,000 commission fee excluding VAT\, in
 clusive of artist fee\, travel\, accommodation and production costs. Appli
 cants must have a VAT number in Europe. No application fee.\nDuration: Jan
 uary to September 2027\, with final presentations in September 2027\n\nMor
 e: https://waag.org/en/article/open-call-creative-interventions-trust-me-i
 m-airtist/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://waag.org/en/article/open-call-creative-interventions-trust-me-i
 m-airtist/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Europe. Engaging with a specific AI Enabler includes an on-site vi
 sit to its technology partner: CERTH (Greece)\, The Audience Agency (Warri
 ngton\, UK)\, the Centre of Excellence for Research & Innovation (Cyprus)\
 , Aristotle University of Thessaloniki (Greece)\, or CAUSA Creation (Austr
 ia). Final presentations September 2027.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:ual-storytelling-research-fellows-2026@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261101T195719Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260911
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260912
SUMMARY:Deadline: UAL AKO Storytelling Institute: Storytelling Research Fel
 lows 2026 (six posts)
DESCRIPTION:The UAL AKO Storytelling Institute\, with the Power of Pop Fund
 \, is recruiting six Storytelling Research Fellows to run independent rese
 arch projects on how storytelling in popular culture shapes attitudes\, be
 haviour and public discourse. Fellows work across media including televisi
 on\, film\, gaming\, podcasts and digital culture\, are supported by the I
 nstitute's Head of Research and a peer cohort\, and produce actionable fin
 dings for creative practitioners\, funders and social-impact organisations
 \, presented at a closing public symposium. ELIGIBILITY: experienced resea
 rchers with a PhD or equivalent research expertise gained academically\, i
 ndependently or through professional practice\, with a track record of del
 ivering high-quality research and working with external partners. Practice
 -based and non-academic researchers are explicitly in scope. APPLICATION: 
 CV\, record of publications\, a research proposal of 1\,000 to 1\,200 word
 s\, and a supporting statement including at least one example of a researc
 h project you designed and delivered. Applications via the UAL jobs portal
 \; closes 11 September 2026.\n\nOrganisation: University of the Arts Londo
 n (UAL AKO Storytelling Institute)\, with the Power of Pop Fund\nLocation:
  High Holborn\, London\, United Kingdom (hybrid)\nAward: GBP 48\,563 per a
 nnum pro rata (around GBP 19\,425 for the part-time fellowship hours)\, pl
 us UAL benefits. No application fee.\nDuration: Nine-month fellowship\, pa
 rt-time\, culminating in a public symposium\n\nMore: https://jobs.arts.ac.
 uk/job/12629-storytelling-research-fellows/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://jobs.arts.ac.uk/job/12629-storytelling-research-fellows/?utm_so
 urce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:High Holborn\, London\, United Kingdom (hybrid)
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:arts-council-england-music-growth-fund-small-strand-2026@artificialnouv
 eau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261101T195719Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260903
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260904
SUMMARY:Deadline: Arts Council England: Music Growth Fund\, GBP 1\,000-40\,
 000 strand
DESCRIPTION:The Music Growth Fund is Arts Council England's new investment 
 programme for growth in new music\, meaning original live performance\, so
 und recording or musical work recently or newly created or performed by li
 ving UK-based artists and music creators. It sits inside the wider GBP 45 
 million Music Growth Package. WHO IT IS FOR: music festivals\, music manag
 ers\, music promoters\, music publishers\, record labels\, recording studi
 os\, rehearsal spaces and grassroots music venues\, plus established secto
 r bodies and other sector support organisations that support them. Each ap
 plicant type has its own Applicant Specific Guidance on the Music Growth P
 ackage pages. IMPORTANT: this is business and organisational investment\, 
 not a grant for individual artists to make work. Individual artists\, band
 s\, producers and artist collectives should instead look at the separate M
 usic Creators Growth Fund (GBP 10\,000-20\,000)\, whose guidance lands 1 S
 eptember 2026 with the portal opening 1 October 2026. WHAT TO PROPOSE: how
  investment will be used to grow music businesses and/or the wider music i
 ndustry\, and enhance opportunities for artists and music creators\, indus
 try professionals\, music fans and new audiences. Growth can mean increase
 d reach\, stronger business models\, improved sustainability\, skills deve
 lopment or expanded capacity\, and is judged in the context of each applic
 ant's own scale and ambitions. DECISION CRITERIA: potential for growth\, e
 ffective use of funds\, contribution to a fairer and more inclusive music 
 industry\, and contribution to a climate resilient music industry. BEFORE 
 YOU START: you need an approved profile on the Arts Council grants managem
 ent system before you can submit\, so allow time for that. Arts Council re
 serves the right to change the programme\, so check you are on the latest 
 guidance. THIS STRAND: GBP 1\,000 to GBP 40\,000\, with a GBP 3\,000\,000 
 annual budget for 2026/27 and a 25 percent cash match requirement. ROUNDS 
 FOR 2026/27 (GBP 1\,000-40\,000 strand): Round 1 opens 6 August and closes
  3 September 2026\, decision 28 October. Round 2 opens 4 September and clo
 ses 15 October 2026\, decision 28 October. Round 3 opens 16 October and cl
 oses 26 November 2026\, decision 3 February 2027. Round 4 opens 27 Novembe
 r 2026 and closes 7 January 2027\, decision 9 March 2027. ROUNDS FOR THE G
 BP 40\,001-100\,000 strand: Round 1 opens 7 September and closes 1 October
  2026\, decision 30 November. Round 2 opens 23 November 2026 and closes 15
  January 2027\, decision 8 March 2027.\n\nOrganisation: Arts Council Engla
 nd\nLocation: England. Supports new music by living\, UK-based artists and
  music creators.\nAward: GBP 1\,000 to GBP 40\,000\, from a total annual b
 udget of GBP 3\,000\,000 for this strand in 2026/27. You must find at leas
 t 25 percent of the total project budget as CASH match funding. No applica
 tion fee.\nDuration: Four rounds across 2026/27\; the next closes 3 Septem
 ber 2026\n\nMore: https://www.artscouncil.org.uk/music-growth-package/musi
 c-growth-fund-general-guidance?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_m
 edium=referral
URL:https://www.artscouncil.org.uk/music-growth-package/music-growth-fund-g
 eneral-guidance?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:England. Supports new music by living\, UK-based artists and music
  creators.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:unlimited-partner-awards-2026-27@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261101T195719Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20260928
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20260929
SUMMARY:Deadline: Unlimited: Partner Awards for Disabled Artists 2026/27 (U
 K)
DESCRIPTION:Unlimited\, the UK's flagship commissioning body for disability
  arts\, opens eleven Partner Awards for 2026/27 delivered with named partn
 er organisations including the Sainsbury Centre\, Norfolk & Norwich Festiv
 al\, Without Walls\, Cryptic\, Scottish Sculpture Workshop and Sherman The
 atre. The awards fund ambitious new work by disabled artists across theatr
 e\, performance\, digital media\, music and visual arts. WHAT YOU GET: awa
 rds of GBP 15\,000 to GBP 40\,000 (the Wales award is GBP 20\,000)\, total
 ling GBP 280\,000 across England\, Scotland and Wales\, supporting R&D thr
 ough to presentation and touring with a named partner. ELIGIBILITY: applic
 ants must be a disabled artist or lead a disabled-led company\, be based i
 n England\, Scotland or Wales\, be 18 or older\, and not be in full-time e
 ducation. ACCESS: application materials are provided in audio\, BSL\, Easy
  Read\, large print\, Scottish Gaelic and Welsh\, and access support for a
 pplying is available via commissions@weareunlimited.org.uk. DEADLINE: 28 S
 eptember 2026 at midday.\n\nOrganisation: Unlimited\nLocation: England\, S
 cotland and Wales\, UK. Open to disabled artists and disabled-led companie
 s based there.\nAward: Eleven awards totalling GBP 280\,000: eight awards 
 of GBP 15\,000 to GBP 40\,000 in England\, two awards of GBP 15\,000 to GB
 P 40\,000 in Scotland\, and one award of GBP 20\,000 in Wales. No applicat
 ion fee.\nDuration: Award period 2026/27\; supports R&D through to present
 ation and touring\, varying per award.\n\nMore: https://weareunlimited.org
 .uk/opportunities/apply/partner-awards-2026-27/?utm_source=artificialnouve
 augrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://weareunlimited.org.uk/opportunities/apply/partner-awards-2026-2
 7/?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:England\, Scotland and Wales\, UK. Open to disabled artists and di
 sabled-led companies based there.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:braid-creative-commissions-a-promise-in-return-2027@artificialnouveau.g
 ithub.io
DTSTAMP:20261101T195719Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261102
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261103
SUMMARY:Deadline: BRAID Creative Commissions 2027: A Promise in Return (Res
 ponsible AI\, GBP 8\,000-12\,000\, Edinburgh Art Festival)
DESCRIPTION:BRAID\, the UKRI AHRC-funded Responsible AI programme at the Un
 iversity of Edinburgh\, is commissioning around seven creative works that 
 treat Responsible AI as a question of relationships\, commitments and obli
 gations rather than a technical checklist or a set of rules\, under the ti
 tle A Promise in Return. WHO: UK-based creative practitioners in any mediu
 m: artists\, designers\, musicians and performers\, architects and urban d
 esigners\, craftspeople and artisans\, writers and poets. Full-time underg
 raduate and taught postgraduate students are not eligible\; PhD students m
 ay apply if registered as self-employed freelancers. WHAT YOU GET: a commi
 ssion budget of GBP 8\,000 to 12\,000\, production support from Inspace an
 d the Institute for Design Informatics\, and presentation during the Edinb
 urgh Art Festival in August 2027 with the Edinburgh Futures Institute. APP
 LICATION: online form (written or an eight-minute video application) plus 
 a single PDF of portfolio\, CV and budget emailed to braid@ed.ac.uk\; late
  submissions are not accepted. DEADLINE: Monday 2 November 2026 at 17:00 G
 MT.\n\nOrganisation: BRAID (Bridging Responsible AI Divides)\, University 
 of Edinburgh\, with Inspace\, the Institute for Design Informatics and the
  Edinburgh Futures Institute\nLocation: UK. For UK-based creative practiti
 oners\; work is presented in Edinburgh during the Edinburgh Art Festival\,
  August 2027.\nAward: Around seven commissions with budgets of GBP 8\,000 
 to 12\,000 each\, inclusive of VAT and all costs. No application fee.\nDur
 ation: Projects run January to August 2027\, presented during the Edinburg
 h Art Festival in August 2027\, with possible further displays through 202
 8.\n\nMore: https://braiduk.org/creative-commissions?utm_source=artificial
 nouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://braiduk.org/creative-commissions?utm_source=artificialnouveaugr
 antdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:UK. For UK-based creative practitioners\; work is presented in Edi
 nburgh during the Edinburgh Art Festival\, August 2027.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:daiwa-foundation-small-grants-march-2027@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261101T195719Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20270331
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20270401
SUMMARY:Deadline: Daiwa Foundation Small Grants\, March 2027 round (GBP 2\,
 000-9\,000 for UK-Japan projects)
DESCRIPTION:The Daiwa Foundation Small Grants support projects that promote
  interaction between the UK and Japan in any field\, including arts exchan
 ges\, exhibitions\, conferences\, grassroots and educational exchanges and
  research travel\, with travel between the two countries as the main focus
 . New initiatives are encouraged\, and gender equality and SDG themes are 
 welcome\; continuing or regular events (such as an annual festival edition
 ) are unlikely to be funded without a strong reason or special occasion. N
 OT ELIGIBLE: commissions of artworks\, capital costs\, consumables\, salar
 ies or professional fees\, tuition and commercial activity. WHAT YOU GET: 
 GBP 2\,000 to 9\,000 (most awards GBP 3\,000-4\,000)\, with flights funded
  up to GBP 1\,200 each and subsistence up to GBP 100 per person per day\, 
 plus GBP 50 extra per flight for environmental projects in Japan. APPLICAT
 ION: read the Application Notes\, then apply via the Foundation's online p
 ortal (separate forms for UK- and Japan-based applicants)\; advice from gr
 ants@dajf.org.uk. Decisions by late May or early June 2027. DEADLINE: 31 M
 arch 2027 (the following round closes 30 September).\n\nOrganisation: Daiw
 a Anglo-Japanese Foundation\nLocation: For individuals\, societies\, assoc
 iations and other bodies in the UK or Japan running projects that connect 
 the two countries.\nAward: GBP 2\,000 to 9\,000 (typical awards GBP 3\,000
 -4\,000)\; flights up to GBP 1\,200 each and subsistence up to GBP 100 per
  person per day. A contribution\, not full funding. No application fee.\nD
 uration: Per project\; activity can start from late May or early June 2027
 .\n\nMore: https://dajf.org.uk/grants-awards-and-prizes/daiwa-foundation-s
 mall-grants?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://dajf.org.uk/grants-awards-and-prizes/daiwa-foundation-small-gra
 nts?utm_source=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:For individuals\, societies\, associations and other bodies in the
  UK or Japan running projects that connect the two countries.
END:VEVENT
BEGIN:VEVENT
UID:cove-park-awarded-residencies-2027-2028@artificialnouveau.github.io
DTSTAMP:20261101T195719Z
DTSTART;VALUE=DATE:20261211
DTEND;VALUE=DATE:20261212
SUMMARY:Deadline: Cove Park Awarded Residencies 2027-2028 (Scotland\, UK\, 
 Central and South America and diasporas)
DESCRIPTION:Cove Park's biennial call for fully funded Awarded Residencies 
 at its 50-acre rural residency site in Argyll\, on the west coast of Scotl
 and\, programmed between May 2027 and December 2028. Residencies run from 
 2 weeks up to 12 weeks (average four weeks) and can be split across the tw
 o years around caring or work commitments\; international residents can co
 unt slow-travel time as part of the funded period. Open to artists\, creat
 ive practitioners\, writers\, translators and researchers in all art forms
  and at every career stage\, including collectives and collaborative group
 s\; no formal qualification needed. Priorities include work responding to 
 climate breakdown\, sound artists and musicians (new Sound Studio)\, and w
 ork on home\, place\, language (Scots\, Gaelic\, BSL)\, displacement and b
 elonging. No final presentation is required. ELIGIBILITY: based in the UK\
 , Central America or South America\; Central and South American diaspora a
 rtists based in Europe or North America\; or born in Scotland and living e
 lsewhere. Anyone in full- or part-time formal education on 11 December 202
 6 is not eligible. SUPPORT: GBP 775 per week fee\; travel allowance (Scotl
 and GBP 65 to 100 Central Belt\, up to GBP 200 Borders/Highlands/Islands\;
  rest of UK up to GBP 250\; Europe up to GBP 400\; other international up 
 to GBP 1\,250)\; materials up to GBP 150\; visa support\; private self-cat
 ering accommodation (suitable for collaborators and those attending with c
 hildren) and private studios. An Application Access Bursary (e.g. to pay a
  support worker) is available for applicants facing barriers due to disabi
 lity or neurodivergence\; request it from Alex Marrs by 30 November 2026. 
 Video or audio applications accepted. Applicants are asked not to use gene
 rative AI in their application. APPLY via the online form: 500-word statem
 ent\, 100-word bio\, CV\, up to 8 images or video/audio links or writing s
 amples. SELECTION: shortlisting by the programme team and former residents
 \; shortlist notified by 12 February 2027\; Zoom interviews the week of 22
  February 2027. Contact: Alex Marrs\, alex@covepark.org. DEADLINE: Friday 
 11 December 2026\, 5pm GMT.\n\nOrganisation: Cove Park\nLocation: Cove Par
 k\, Peaton Hill\, Argyll and Bute\, Scotland (in person\, private self-cat
 ering accommodation and studio). Open to people based in the UK\, Central 
 or South America\, Central and South American diaspora artists based in Eu
 rope or North America\, and Scotland-born artists living anywhere. Student
 s in formal education are not eligible.\nAward: Fully funded: GBP 775 per 
 week fee (VAT inclusive\, freelance)\, travel allowance (Scotland GBP 65 t
 o 200\, rest of UK up to GBP 250\, Europe up to GBP 400\, other internatio
 nal up to GBP 1\,250)\, materials up to GBP 150 for studio-based practitio
 ners\, private accommodation and studio\, visa and access support. Applica
 tion Access Bursary available (request by 30 November 2026). No applicatio
 n fee.\nDuration: 2 to 12 weeks (average 4)\, between May 2027 and Decembe
 r 2028\n\nMore: https://covepark.org/awarded-residencies-2027-2028/?utm_so
 urce=artificialnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
URL:https://covepark.org/awarded-residencies-2027-2028/?utm_source=artifici
 alnouveaugrantdesk&utm_medium=referral
LOCATION:Cove Park\, Peaton Hill\, Argyll and Bute\, Scotland (in person\, 
 private self-catering accommodation and studio). Open to people based in t
 he UK\, Central or South America\, Central and South American diaspora art
 ists based in Europe or North America\, and Scotland-born artists living a
 nywhere. Students in formal education are not eligible.
END:VEVENT
END:VCALENDAR
